---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { profiles, version, repository, docHref } from '../../lib/site';
const families = [
  { name: 'Chrome', match: /^chrome/, platform: 'Desktop and Android' },
  { name: 'Firefox', match: /^firefox/, platform: 'Desktop' },
  { name: 'Safari', match: /^safari/, platform: 'Desktop and iOS' },
  { name: 'Edge', match: /^edge/, platform: 'Desktop' },
  { name: 'Tor Browser', match: /^tor/, platform: 'Desktop; profile only' },
  { name: 'OkHttp', match: /^okhttp/, platform: 'Android HTTP client' },
];
---

<Doc
  title="Browser and client profiles"
  slug="profiles"
  section="Usage"
  description="Explore the curlimp profile catalog for Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge, Tor and OkHttp. Learn profile selection, environment priority and header overrides."
>
  <h2>Choose a profile</h2>
  <p>
    Chrome 150 is the default. Put an exact profile name before the URL, or use
    <code>--impersonate</code> to select a different compiled profile.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={
      'curlimp firefox147 -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/200\ncurlimp -sS safari2601 -I https://httpbin.org/status/301\ncurlimp --impersonate chrome131_android -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/502'
    }
  />
  <p>
    Flags may appear on either side of the positional profile. Names must match
    exactly; <a href={docHref('completion')}>Zsh autocomplete</a> expands any
    matching prefix with Tab. Use <code>--url chrome150</code> or
    <code>-- chrome150</code> if that name is a hostname instead of a profile.
  </p>
  <p>
    The profile platform describes the client being impersonated, independently
    of your host OS. You can select an Android profile from a Linux or macOS
    binary.
  </p>
  <h2>
    Included profiles <span class="profile-count">{profiles.length}</span>
  </h2>
  <p>
    This table is generated from the CLI's{' '}
    <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/src/profiles.txt`}>profile catalog</a> for
    v{version}. Names alone do not establish real-browser fingerprint
    equivalence.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Family</th>
        <th>Target</th>
        <th>Profile IDs</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {families.map((family) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{family.name}</td>
          <td>{family.platform}</td>
          <td>
            {profiles
              .filter((p) => family.match.test(p))
              .sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, 'en', { numeric: true }))
              .map((p) => (
                <>
                  <code data-profile={p}>{p}</code>{' '}
                </>
              ))}
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The Tor profile does not connect to the Tor network or provide anonymity.
    Proxy routing is a separate setting.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      <code>okhttp4_android</code> is an HTTP client preset whose bundled
      headers include a desktop Safari User-Agent. Inspect or override its
      headers before use; catalog availability does not establish OkHttp
      fingerprint fidelity.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <h2>Check the installed engine</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={'curli --list-profiles\ncurli --version'} />
  <p>
    <code>--list-profiles</code> validates the catalog against the loaded
    engine, without making network requests. A profile update requires a
    matching engine build; editing a name in the catalog does not create new
    native settings.
  </p>
  <h2>Selection order</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      The last explicit selection: a positional profile,
      <code>--impersonate</code>, or <code>--no-impersonate</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <code>CURL_IMPERSONATE</code> environment variable.
    </li>
    <li>
      The release default, <code>chrome150</code>.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The default is pinned to the release. It does not silently follow a newly
    released browser.
  </p>
  <h2>Control browser headers</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    code={
      '# Apply profile transport settings without its default headers\ncurli --impersonate chrome150:no -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/200'
    }
  />
  <p>
    The <code>:yes</code>/<code>:no</code> suffix controls profile headers.
    Explicit selection enables headers if the suffix is omitted.{' '}
    <code>CURL_IMPERSONATE_HEADERS=no</code> applies to environment/default
    selection. Suppressing profile headers does not disable automatic
    decompression.
  </p>
  <h2>Disable impersonation</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    code={'curli --no-impersonate -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/200'}
  />
  <p>
    This disables profile application, including environment defaults. It still
    uses the packaged modified native engine; it is not a switch to the system
    curl executable or a promise of stock curl fingerprint identity. Response
    decoding becomes opt-in with <code>--compressed</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read <a href={docHref('architecture')}>how profiles reach the engine</a> and
    the <a href={docHref('compatibility')}>validation boundaries</a>.
  </p>
</Doc>
